{"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Performance"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/performance"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Performance","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"kind":"article","primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"ScreenStates’ pitch is “small, dependency-free, drop into any screen.” This article covers the three ways that’s kept measurable rather than just assumed: a CI-enforced size budget, an audited concurrency guarantee, and a documented rendering characteristic.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"anchor":"The-binary-size-budget","type":"heading","text":"The binary-size budget"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"CI builds ","type":"text"},{"code":"swift build -c release","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" on every push and sums the resulting ","type":"text"},{"code":".o","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" sizes for the ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStates","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" target, failing the build if that total exceeds the budget committed in ","type":"text"},{"code":".github\/binary-size-budget.json","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". There’s no single linked artifact to measure instead — the library product is “automatic,” and nothing in the package’s own build graph links it standalone — so the ","type":"text"},{"code":".o","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" total is the closest deterministic proxy available.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A legitimate feature addition that grows this total past the budget isn’t blocked forever: bump ","type":"text"},{"code":"maxBytes","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in that file as part of the same PR, the same way a dependency lockfile gets updated alongside the change that needed it. The job never bumps it on its own.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"anchor":"The-Task-lifecycle-guarantee","type":"heading","text":"The Task lifecycle guarantee"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"text":"’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadWithRetry","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" each store their running ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in a private ","type":"text"},{"code":"currentTask","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" property, read by ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"deinit","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Every one of those ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s captures ","type":"text"},{"code":"self","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" as ","type":"text"},{"code":"[weak self]","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", not strongly — audited specifically to rule out a ","type":"text"},{"code":"self -> currentTask -> closure -> self","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" retain cycle keeping a store alive after its last external reference is dropped.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In practice, a completed ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" releases its closure’s captures on return regardless of capture style, so this mostly matters for a still-","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"running","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" operation: without ","type":"text"},{"code":"[weak self]","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", a store couldn’t become eligible for deallocation until that operation finished, even once every other reference to it was already gone. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ConcurrencyAndCancellation"},{"text":" for the rest of the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" lifecycle — cancellation, overlapping calls, and the automatic cancel-on-","type":"text"},{"code":"deinit","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" safety net this guarantee supports.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"anchor":"Re-render-characteristics-of-the-default-placeholders","type":"heading","text":"Re-render characteristics of the default placeholders"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/isRefreshing"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/refreshError"},{"text":" only ever go active once ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/refresh(_:)"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/refreshCollection(_:)"},{"text":" have confirmed ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is already ","type":"text"},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — both fall back to ","type":"text"},{"code":"load(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" otherwise, which never touch either property. That means ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" can never be observed alongside ","type":"text"},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", or ","type":"text"},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so none of the three default placeholders can ever be on screen mid-refresh.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A parent view that reads ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (via ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView"},{"text":") and ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in the same ","type":"text"},{"code":"body","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — the pattern ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"},{"text":" uses for a refresh toast — still has its ","type":"text"},{"code":"body","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" re-evaluated whenever either changes, same as any ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" property read in a SwiftUI ","type":"text"},{"code":"body","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". This guarantee is about ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"which","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" placeholder that re-evaluation could ever affect, not about avoiding the re-evaluation itself: since a default placeholder is only ever on screen while ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are both inactive, that re-evaluation never has a placeholder